<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Written Homework Exercises</title>

    <page>
        <!-- Q1 Section 1.1 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    For each of the given systems, state (i) the number of
                    equations, (ii) the number of unknowns, and (iii) whether or
                    not the system is linear.
                    For (iii), provide written justification for your
                    conclusion.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>
          \begin{cases}
x_1 \amp - \amp 3x_2 \amp + \amp 4x_3 \amp - \amp x_4 \amp = \amp 2 \\
x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2  \amp + \amp x_3 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp 5 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>
          \begin{cases}
x \amp - \amp 5y \amp + \amp 2z \amp = \amp 2 \\
3x \amp + \amp 2y \amp - \amp 4z \amp = \amp 7 \\
9x \amp + \amp 3y \amp - \amp z \amp = \amp -4 \\
-2x \amp - \amp y \amp + \amp 8z \amp = \amp 0 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>
          \begin{cases}
x^2 \amp - \amp 2xy \amp + \amp y^3 \amp = \amp 9 \\
\sin(x) \amp + \amp e^y \amp - \amp 4y \amp = \amp 0 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q2 Section 1.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Give an example of a matrix in reduced row echelon form that
                    describes a linear system having the stated properties.
                    For each, write one short sentence that explains why your
                    example meets the stated criteria.
                    If it is not possible to find such an example, explain why
                    not.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        a linear system having 4 equations and 2 unknowns and
                        having exactly one solution.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        a linear system having 3 equations and 3 unknowns and
                        having no solution.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        a linear system having 2 equations and 5 unknowns and
                        having infinitely many solutions.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        a linear system having 3 equations and 5 unknowns and
                        having exactly one solution.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        a linear system having 4 equations and 4 unknowns and
                        having exactly one solution.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q3 Section 1.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A given linear system has been represented by an augmented
                    matrix and the process of Gaussian elimination has begun.
                    The current state of the process is shown in the augmented
                    matrix below.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Execute the next <alert>three steps</alert> (that is, the
                    next three row operations) of Gaussian elimination, and
                    clearly state the three row operations you used, employing
                    notation like we've been using in class.
                    Your work should show at least two additional matrices
                    beyond the one stated here.
                    <md>
                        \left[
    \begin{array}{rrr|r}
      1 \amp 5 \amp -3 \amp 6 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \\
      0 \amp -2 \amp 7 \amp 0 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q4 Section 1.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the linear system:
                    <md>
                        \begin{array}{rcrcrcr}
-2x_1 \amp + \amp x_2 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp -4 \\
3x_1 \amp \ \amp \  \amp + \amp 3x_3 \amp = \amp 9 \\
-x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2  \amp - \amp 5x_3 \amp = \amp -7
\end{array}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the augmented matrix that corresponds to the given
                        system.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Work by hand to find the reduced row echelon form of the
                        augmented matrix.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Identify which variables are basic and which are free.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Describe the solution space of the system, using a
                        parametric description if there are infinitely many
                        solutions.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q5 Section 1.3 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    For each of the following linear systems of equations, (i)
                    represent the system with an augmented matrix, (ii) use
                    \emph{Sage} to find the RREF of the matrix of the linear
                    system, and report the RREF in your written summary, and
                    (iii) describe the solution space of the system in as much
                    detail as you can, using parametric form if there are
                    infinitely many solutions.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>
          \begin{cases}
x \amp - \amp 5y \amp + \amp 2z \amp = \amp 2 \\
3x \amp + \amp 2y \amp - \amp 4z \amp = \amp 7 \\
9x \amp + \amp 3y \amp - \amp z \amp = \amp -4 \\
-2x \amp - \amp y \amp + \amp 8z \amp = \amp 0  \\
\end{cases}
</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>
          \begin{cases}
x_1 \amp - \amp 3x_2 \amp + \amp 4x_3 \amp - \amp x_4 \amp = \amp 2 \\
x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2 \amp + \amp x_3 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp 5 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>
          \begin{cases}
x_1 \amp - \amp 3x_2 \amp + \amp 4x_3 \amp - \amp x_4 \amp = \amp 2 \\
x_1 \amp - \amp 2x_2  \amp + \amp x_3 \amp \ \amp \ \amp = \amp 5 \\
-2x_1 \amp + \amp 1x_2  \amp - \amp 3x_3 \amp + \amp x_4 \amp = \amp -1 \\
-x_1 \amp - \amp 4x_2  \amp + \amp 2x_3 \amp + \amp 5x_4 \amp = \amp 0 \\
\end{cases}
</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q6 Section 1.4 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    For each of the four scenarios below, give an example of an
                    augmented matrix in RREF that satisfies the stated criteria,
                    or explain why such a matrix is not possible.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For each example you provide, (i) circle the pivot
                    locations, (ii) make each non-pivot column have at least one
                    non-zero entry, and (iii) write one sentence to say why the
                    system is consistent or inconsistent.
                    In addition, if the corresponding linear system is
                    consistent, describe the solution space for your example.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        An augmented matrix with four rows, three columns, and
                        two pivots for which the corresponding linear system is
                        consistent.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        An augmented matrix with three rows, five columns, and
                        three pivots for which the corresponding linear system
                        is inconsistent.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        An augmented matrix that corresponds to a linear system
                        of 4 equations in 3 variables that has infinitely many
                        solutions.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        An augmented matrix that corresponds to a linear system
                        of 3 equations in 5 unknown variables that has a unique
                        solution.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q7 Section 2.1 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the vectors <m>\uvec</m>, <m>\vvec</m>,
                    <m>\xvec</m>, <m>\yvec</m>, and <m>\zvec</m> pictured in
                    this figure:
                </p>

                <image source="images/HW-lin-comb-nonstd.eps" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>4 vectors in a plane.</shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    Notice that while there is no scale on the axes, the vectors
                    <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m> generate a "grid", and the
                    tips of the vectors <m>\xvec</m>, <m>\yvec</m>, and
                    <m>\zvec</m> lie at intersection points on the grid.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For each of the vectors <m>\xvec</m>, <m>\yvec</m>, and
                        <m>\zvec</m>, express the vector as a linear combination
                        of <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>.
                        Clearly state each linear combination by writing an
                        equation such as
                        <m>\xvec = \underline{\hspace{0.25in}}\uvec +  \underline{\hspace{0.25in}}\vvec</m>.
                        After you do this for all three vectors, write a
                        sentence that explains how you determined the weights in
                        your linear combinations.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Sketch and label the vector
                        <m>\wvec = 3\uvec - \vvec</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What geometric figure is formed by the tips of all
                        vectors of the form <m>{\uvec + t\vvec}</m>, when
                        <m>t</m> is allowed to be any real number?
                        (Hint: you might plot some points that correspond to
                        several different values of <m>t</m> that you choose.)
                        Write a careful sentence or two that explains your
                        conclusion and how you determined it.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q8 Section 2.1 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the linear system
                    <md>
                        \begin{array}{rcrcr}
2x \amp - \amp 3y \amp = \amp 13 \\
3x \amp + \amp y  \amp = \amp 3 \\
\end{array}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Solve the system and describe the solution space fully.
                        If there are infinitely many solutions, describe them in
                        parametric form.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\uvec = \twovec{2}{3}</m> and
                        <m>\vvec = \twovec{-3}{1}</m>.
                        How can you rewrite the given linear system above as
                        asking a question about a linear combination of
                        <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\bvec = \twovec{13}{3}</m>.
                        Is <m>\bvec</m> a linear combination of <m>\uvec</m> and
                        <m>\vvec</m>?
                        Why or why not?
                        If yes, give the weights that make <m>\bvec</m> a linear
                        combination of <m>\uvec</m> and <m>\vvec</m>; if no,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q9 Section 2.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 2</m> matrix whose
                    columns are the vectors <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>:
                    <m>A = [ \vvec_1 \ \ \vvec_2]</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What are the dimensions of the vectors <m>\vvec_1</m>
                        and <m>\vvec_2</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the matrix-vector product <m>A \twovec10</m> as a
                        linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                        Similarly, write <m>A \twovec01</m> as a linear
                        combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose we are now given some specific information about
                        certain matrix-vector products.
                        Specifically, say we are given
                        <md>
                            A \twovec10 = \threevec{2}{-1}{4} \ \mbox{and} \ A \twovec01 = \threevec{0}{3}{-1}.
                        </md>
                        What is the matrix <m>A</m>?
                        Why?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine <m>A \twovec{-3}{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q10 Section 2.3 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the vectors <m>\uvec = \threevec{1}{0}{-1}</m>,
                    <m>\vvec = \threevec{0}{-1}{1}</m>, and
                    <m>\wvec = \threevec{2}{-3}{1}</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is <m>\bvec = \threevec{-5}{7}{-3}</m> in Span
                        <m>\{ \uvec, \vvec, \wvec \}</m>?
                        Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is <m>\cvec = \threevec{-5}{7}{-2}</m> in Span
                        <m>\{ \uvec, \vvec, \wvec \}</m>?
                        Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Do the vectors <m>\uvec, \vvec, \wvec</m> span all of
                        <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>?
                        Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is an important observation to make about the
                        relationship among <m>\uvec, \vvec, \wvec</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Give a careful written description of Span
                        <m>\{ \uvec, \vvec, \wvec \}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>A</m> is the matrix
                    <m>A = [ \vvec_1 \ \ \vvec_2 \ \ \vvec_3]</m> where
                    <md>
                        \vvec_1 = \threevec111, \ \ \vvec_2 = \threevec{\phantom{-}2}{-1}{\phantom{-}0}, \ \ \vvec_3 = \threevec{-1}{\phantom{-}3}{\phantom{-}2}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If
                        <m>\xvec = \threevec{\phantom{-}4}{-2}{\phantom{-}3}</m>,
                        write <m>A\xvec</m> in two different ways: (i) as a
                        linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>,
                        and <m>\vvec_3</m>, and (ii) as a single vector that you
                        find by hand.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>\bvec = \threevec{\phantom{-}5}{-7}{-3}</m>,
                        determine all solutions to the equation
                        <m>A\xvec = \bvec</m>.
                        Clearly show how this problem can be turned into a
                        system of equations (don't just write down an augmented
                        matrix), and solve.
                        If there are infinitely many solutions, express them in
                        parametric vector form.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is <m>\cvec =  \threevec{\phantom{-}5}{-7}{-2}</m> a
                        linear combination of <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>,
                        and <m>\vvec_3</m>?
                        Clearly show how this question can be turned into a
                        system of linear equations and then explain your answer.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Q12 Section 2.3 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose that we have the following collection of vectors in
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>:
                    <md>
                        {\vvec_1 = \fourvec{1}{1}{1}{1}, 
          \vvec_2 = \fourvec{\phantom{-}1}{\phantom{-}1}{-1}{-1}, 
          \vvec_3 = \fourvec{\phantom{-}1}{-1}{\phantom{-}1}{-1}}
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Give an example of a nonzero vector $\wvec$ that is not
                        equal to <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, or
                        <m>\vvec_3</m> that belongs to the span of
                        <m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3 \}</m>.
                        Write a sentence to say why your vector has the desired
                        property.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Explain why Span <m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3 \}</m>
                        is not <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> using pivots.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find a vector <m>\vvec_4</m> so that <m>\vvec_4</m> is
                        not in Span <m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3 \}</m>, and
                        clearly justify why your vector has this property.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What can you say about Span
                        <m>\{ \vvec_1, \vvec_2, \vvec_3, \vvec_4 \}</m>?
                        Why?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 13, Section 2.3, 2.4 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let the vectors <m>{\vec u}</m>, <m>{\vec v}</m>,
                    <m>{\vec w}</m>, and <m>{\vec z}</m> be given by
                    <md>
                        {\vec u} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}2 \\ 1 \\ -1\end{array} \right], \ {\vec v} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}-3 \\ -1 \\ 2\end{array} \right], \ {\vec w} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{array} \right], \ {\vec z} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}1 \\ 2 \\ 3\end{array} \right]
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        For each of the following sets of vectors, is the set
                        linearly dependent or linearly independent?
                        Give a clear justification of each conclusion, including
                        any relevant computations you use.
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{ {\vec u}, {\vec v} \}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{ {\vec u}, {\vec v}, {\vec w} \}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{ {\vec u}, {\vec v}, {\vec w}, {\vec z} \}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Can you find a set of <em>exactly three</em> vectors
                        among <m>{\vec u}</m>, <m>{\vec v}</m>, <m>{\vec w}</m>,
                        and <m>{\vec z}</m> that is linearly independent?
                        If so, do so with justification; if not, explain why
                        not.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Of the three sets of vectors in (a), which set(s) span
                        all of <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{3}</m>?
                        Write carefully to explain your conclusions.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 14, Section 2.3/2.4 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>k</m> is an unknown parameter and consider
                    the set of vectors
                    <md>
                        {\vec v}_{1} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}1 \\ 1 \\ -1\end{array} \right], \ {\vec v}_{2} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}2 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{array} \right], \ {\vec v}_{3} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}3 \\ 1 \\ k\end{array} \right]
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For what value(s) of <m>k</m> is the set
                        <m>\{ {\vec v}_{1}, {\vec v}_{2}, {\vec v}_{3} \}</m>
                        linearly dependent?
                        Why?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For what value(s) of <m>k</m> is Span
                        <m>\{ {\vec v}_{1}, {\vec v}_{2}, {\vec v}_{3} \} = {\mathbb{R}}^{3}</m>
                        Why?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How are your responses to (a) and (b) connected to one
                        another?
                        (There are several important things you can say here.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 15, Section 2.5/2.6 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>S : \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> be the
                    linear transformation that reflects each input vector across
                    the line <m>y = x</m>, and let
                    <m>R : \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> be the
                    transformation that rotates each input vector by
                    <m>90^{\circ}</m> clockwise about the origin.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine the matrices <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> such that
                        <m>S({\vec x}) = B{\vec x}</m> and
                        <m>R({\vec x}) = C{\vec x}</m>.
                        For each, write a sentence to explain your reasoning.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Consider the new transformation <m>T</m> that is defined
                        by <m>T({\vec x}) = (R \circ S)({\vec x})</m>.
                        Determine the matrix <m>M</m> for which
                        <m>T({\vec x}) = M{\vec x}</m>.
                        Clearly show your work and thinking.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For the transformation <m>T</m> defined in (b) as the
                        composition of <m>R</m> and <m>S</m>, what is the effect
                        of <m>T</m> on input vectors?
                        (For instance, you might see if you can determine if
                        <m>T</m> generates a rotation around the origin by a
                        certain angle, a reflection across a certain line, or
                        some other geometric effect.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create the new transformation <m>Q</m> that is defined
                        by <m>Q({\vec x}) = (S\circ R)({\vec x})</m>.
                        Are <m>Q</m> and <m>T</m> the same transformation?
                        Explain how you know.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    For each of the following 3 prompts ((a), (b), and (c)), you
                    are given some general information about a collection of
                    vectors, and then asked to consider two statements, <q>I</q>
                    and <q>II</q>, about those vectors.
                    For each, your task is to decide which of them is true.
                    It could be that both are true, only one of them is true,
                    both are false, or there's not enough information to say
                    (about one or both of them).
                </p>

                <p>
                    For each prompt, your task is to (i) make an appropriate
                    conclusion about each of the two statements (is it true,
                    false, or can't say) <em>and</em> (ii) provide clear written
                    justification that explains how you have reasoned from the
                    given information to your conclusion.
                    If you use a proposition from the text, please refer to that
                    clearly by number and by a summary of what the proposition
                    tells you.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Suppose that a set of vectors in <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</m>
                        has fewer vectors than there are entries in each vector.
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The set of vectors is linearly independent.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The set of vectors doesn't span
                            <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </task>

            <task>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Suppose that a set of vectors in <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</m>
                        has more vectors than there are entries in each vector.
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The set of vectors is linearly independent.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The set of vectors doesn't span
                            <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </task>

            <task>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Suppose we have a set of 12 vectors
                        <m>\{{\vec v}_{1}, {\vec v}_{2}, \ldots, {\vec v}_{12}\}</m>
                        in <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{12}</m>; note that we have exactly
                        the same number of vectors as there are components in
                        each vector.
                        In addition, suppose that you know that for some vector
                        <m>{\vec b}</m> in <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{12}</m>,
                        <m>{\vec b}</m> can be written as a linear combination
                        of the vectors <m>{\vec v}_{1}</m>, <m>{\vec v}_{2}</m>,
                        <m>\ldots</m>, <m>{\vec v}_{12}</m> in exactly one way.
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The set of vectors is linearly independent.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The set of vectors doesn't span
                            <m>{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 17 Section 2.5 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>T</m> is the matrix transformation defined
                    by the matrix <m>A</m> and <m>S</m> is the matrix
                    transformation defined by <m>B</m> where
                    <md>
                        A = \left[ \begin{array}{rrr}3 &amp;-1 &amp; 0\\ 1 &amp;2 &amp; 2 \\ -1 &amp; 3 &amp;2\end{array}\right], \,\,\,\,\, B =\left[ \begin{array}{rrr}1 &amp;-1 &amp;0 \\ 2&amp;1 &amp;2\end{array}\right].
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>T : \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{m}</m> what are
                        the values of <m>n</m> and <m>m</m>?
                        What values of <m>n</m> and <m>m</m> are appropriate for
                        the transformation <m>S</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                        <m>T \left( \left[\begin{array}{c}\phantom{-}1 \\ -3 \\ \phantom{-}2\end{array}\right]\right)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                        <m>S\left(\left[\begin{array}{c}-2 \\ \phantom{-}2 \\ \phantom{-}1\end{array}\right]\right)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Evaluate
                        <m>(S\circ T)\left(\left[\begin{array}{c}\phantom{-}1 \\ -3 \\ \phantom{-}2\end{array}\right]\right)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the matrix <m>C</m> that defines the matrix
                        transformation <m>S\circ T</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>T</m> is a matrix transformation such that
                    <m>T : \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}^{3}</m>, and we know
                    that
                    <md>
                        T\left(\left[\begin{array}{c}1 \\ 0\end{array}\right]\right) = \left[ \begin{array}{r}2 \\ 1 \\ -1\end{array} \right] \ \mbox{and}\ T\left(\left[\begin{array}{c}0 \\ 1\end{array}\right]\right) = \left[ \begin{array}{r}-1 \\ 3 \\ 2\end{array} \right].
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the matrix <m>A</m> such that
                        <m>T({\vec x}) = A{\vec x}</m> and hence determine
                        <m>T({\vec v})</m> for
                        <m>{\vec v} = \left[ \begin{array}{r}-3 \\ 4\end{array} \right].</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine and describe all vectors <m>{\vec x}</m> such
                        that
                        <m>T({\vec x}) = \left[ \begin{array}{r}3 \\ 19 \\ 6\end{array} \right]</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        True or false: for any <m>{\vec b}</m> in
                        <m>\mathbb{R}^{3}</m>, we can find a solution
                        <m>{\vec x}</m> to the equation
                        <m>T({\vec x}) = {\vec b}</m>.
                        (Be sure to justify your conclusion.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 19 Section 2.6/3.1 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    This problem asks you to investigate some invertible
                    matrices whose inverse has a special property.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Explain why the <m>2 \times 2</m> identity matrix,
                        <m>I_{2}</m>, is invertible and why its inverse is
                        itself.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find 2 other <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices <m>B</m> and
                        <m>C</m> such that <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, and
                        <m>I_{2}</m> are four different matrices and each matrix
                        has the property that the matrix is invertible and its
                        inverse is itself.
                        Write to explain your thinking for how you found
                        <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Give an example, with justification, of an invertible
                        <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>M</m> such that <m>M</m> is
                        not <m>I_{3}</m> and <m>M^{-1}= M</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 20, Section 3.1 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Determine whether the following statements are true or false
                    and explain your reasoning.
                    If you use a proposition from the text, please refer to that
                    clearly by number and by a summary of what the proposition
                    tells you.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is invertible, then the columns of <m>A</m>
                        are linearly independent.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is a square matrix whose diagonal entries
                        are all nonzero, then <m>A</m> is invertible.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is a square matrix whose diagonal entries
                        are all nonzero, then <m>A</m> is invertible.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is an invertible <m>n\times n</m> matrix,
                        then span of the columns of <m>A</m> is
                        <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is invertible, then there is a nonzero
                        solution to the homogeneous equation
                        <m>A{\vec x} = {\vec 0}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix and the
                        equation <m>A{\vec x}={\vec b}</m> has a solution for
                        every vector <m>{\vec b}</m>, then <m>A</m> is
                        invertible.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Using the figure, determine
                        <m>\{ {\vec a} \}_{\mathcal{B}}</m>,
                        <m>\{ {\vec b} \}_{\mathcal{B}}</m>, and
                        <m>\{ {\vec c} \}_{\mathcal{B}}</m> for the vectors
                        <md>
                            {\vec a} = \left[\begin{array}{c}-6 \\ 0\end{array}\right], \ {\vec b} = \left[\begin{array}{c}0 \\ -6\end{array}\right], \ {\vec c} = \left[\begin{array}{c}4 \\ 5\end{array}\right].
                        </md>
                        Again, write at least one sentence that explains how you
                        determined your results.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 21, Section 3.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the vectors <m>{\vec v}_{1}</m> and
                    <m>{\vec v}_{2}</m> given in the figure below and let
                    <m>\mathcal{B}= \{ {\vec v}_{1}, {\vec v}_{2} \}</m>.
