    <page>
        <!-- Question 22 Section 3.1 and 3.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Provide a justification for your response to each of the
                    following questions.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose you have a linearly independent set of <m>m</m>
                        vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^{m}</m>.
                        Can you guarantee that this set of vectors forms a basis
                        of <m>\mathbb{R}^{m}</m>?
                        Explain.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose you have a set of 10 vectors
                        <m>\{{\vec v}_{1}, {\vec v}_{2}, \ldots, {\vec v}_{10}\}</m>
                        in <m>\mathbb{R}^{10}</m> and every vector in
                        <m>\mathbb{R}^{10}</m> can be written as a linear
                        combination of these 10 vectors.
                        Can you guarantee that this set of vectors forms a basis
                        for <m>\mathbb{R}^{10}</m>?
                        Explain.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>15\times15</m> matrix and that
                        the equation <m>A{\vec x} = {\vec b}</m> has a unique
                        solution for some vector <m>{\vec b}</m>.
                        Can you guarantee that <m>A</m> is invertible?
                        Explain.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Question 23 Section 3.2 -->
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Crystallographers find it convenient to use coordinate
                    systems that are adapted to the specific geometry of a
                    crystal.
                    As a two-dimensional example, consider a layer of graphite
                    in which carbon atoms are arranged in regular hexagons to
                    form the crystalline structure shown below.
                </p>

                <image source="images/ex-graphite.eps"/>
                <p>
                    The origin of the coordinate is at the carbon atom labeled
                    by <q>0.</q> It is convenient to choose the basis
                    <m>{\cal B}</m> defined by the indicated vectors
                    <m>{\vec v}_{1}</m> and <m>{\vec v}_{2}</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Indicate the locations <m>{\vec x}</m> for which
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{{\vec x}\}_{{\cal B}}= \left[\begin{array}{c}1 \\ 0\end{array}\right]</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{{\vec x}\}_{{\cal B}}= \left[\begin{array}{c}0 \\ 1\end{array}\right]</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\{{\vec x}\}_{{\cal B}}= \left[\begin{array}{c}2 \\ 1\end{array}\right]</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                </task>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the coordinates in the basis <m>{\cal B}</m> of the
                        six yellow carbon atoms.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the coordinates of the center of the hexagon
                        labeled <q>C.</q>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the coordinates of the atom labeled <q>1.</q>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
